{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-correctness.json && printf '\\\\n---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-safety_bypass.json && printf '\\\\n---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/r1-completeness_governance.json && printf '\\\\n---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":547337,\"duration_api_ms\":548024,\"num_turns\":23,\"result\":\"Now I have everything I need to produce the review. Let me compose the result.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-extras delivers --restore mode, ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass, and CI workflow wiring. The GET→PUT shape transform (stripping _links/url/checks/required_signatures, coercing enforce_admins to boolean, null required_status_checks → empty PUT-safe shape) is correctly implemented. APPLY_FAIL=0 wrap pattern preserves audit-trail emission on partial failure. required_signatures POST/DELETE sub-resource handling is correct including 404-suppression. Posture-degradation flags fire correctly. The prior_restore_evidence_present awk function scans committed blob, working-tree diff, and git history — R8-SAFE-002 addressed. forward-mode safety check uses path-pinned workflow lookup (.path == '.github/workflows/policy-check-wrapper.yml', not display name), correctly omits &branch= on workflow-runs API. Gate 3 --expected-wrapper-sha validates against actual release-tag SHAs via refs/tags/<tag> resolution. All --restore + forward-mode-flag mutexes are in place. ADOPT-001 §12.8 playbook is concrete and complete with mandatory acknowledgement flags and WARNING/CAUTION callouts. D-047 mirrors D-044 shape, cites 024B-core split, and acknowledges the escape hatch. One CRIT defect found: the CI workflow specifies both paths: and paths-ignore: on the same pull_request event, which GitHub Actions explicitly prohibits. Two MAJ defects: a missing test for the arbitrary-SHA rejection path, and a silent dispatch-note selection flaw in the workflow job that could mis-enforce on multi-slice PRs. Two minor gaps in schema validation and decision record completeness.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-024B-EXTRAS-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"CRIT\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\".github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml specifies both paths: and paths-ignore: under the same pull_request event. GitHub Actions documentation explicitly states: 'You cannot use both the paths and paths-ignore filters for the same event in a workflow.' If GitHub rejects the YAML, the check-invocation-log-entry CI gate never fires for any cohort cascade slice (024C-F), defeating the primary deliverable of 024B-extras.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\".github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 17-22: on.pull_request.paths includes three entries (024[C-Z]*/**/…, branch-protection-log.md, STATUS.md) and on.pull_request.paths-ignore includes docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**. These filters coexist on the same trigger. GitHub Actions API contract forbids this combination. D-047 text describes only paths-ignore; the paths: filter was an undocumented addition. The DISPATCH-NOTE spec also listed branch-protection-log.md and STATUS.md in paths-ignore, not paths — the implementation diverges from spec AND violates the GitHub contract.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If GitHub rejects the workflow YAML, no CI enforcement runs on any 024C-F cohort PR. If GitHub silently ignores paths-ignore, all PRs touching STATUS.md or branch-protection-log.md (including 024A/024B tooling PRs) trigger the workflow unnecessarily; the job-body short-circuit handles this correctly, but behavior is undefined. Either way, the workflow violates the GitHub Actions API contract.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Remove paths-ignore entirely. The paths: 024[C-Z]*/** filter already excludes 024A/024B dispatch-note paths; the job-body short-circuit (checking slice_id against 024A|024B-core|024B-extras|024G) handles STATUS.md/branch-protection-log triggers from tooling PRs. If the explicit paths-ignore bootstrap guard is needed for 024A/024B, use ONLY paths-ignore and remove paths:, relying on the job-body to exit 0 for all non-cohort PRs.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-024B-EXTRAS-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Tests do not cover --expected-wrapper-sha with a SHA that matches no release tag (expected exit 2). DISPATCH-NOTE explicitly specifies 'arbitrary SHA → exit 2' as a required test case. The validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags function emits this error, but no test exercises the path.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Grep for 'expected.wrapper.sha.*arbitrary|arbitrary.*SHA|expected-wrapper-sha.*exit 2' in test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh returns no matches. enable-expected-sha test (line 2066) covers the positive case (valid tag SHA → exit 0). enable-expected-sha-cross-target (line 2146) covers wrong target repo, not wrong SHA. validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags at scripts/enable-required-check.sh:454 exits 2 with 'not found in the release-tag SHA cache' — this path is untested.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A regression to validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags (e.g., a jq expression change that always returns success) would go undetected. Gate 3 integrity relies on this check; a broken check could allow an operator to supply an arbitrary SHA and bypass the release-tag anchoring that Gate 3 enforces.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a test that sets up a tags.json with one tag pointing to SHA '1111…', runs --expected-wrapper-sha 2222… (a SHA not in any tag), and asserts exit 2 with 'not found in the release-tag SHA cache'. Mirror the existing enable-expected-sha fixture structure.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-024B-EXTRAS-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The Resolve dispatch note path step in check-invocation-log-entry.yml uses 'grep … | tail -n1' to select the DISPATCH-NOTE. If a PR modifies files in multiple slice directories, tail -n1 silently picks the last alphabetically, and the CI gate enforces only that slice's cascade-status while the others are unchecked.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\".github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml line 46: dispatch_note=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' \\\\\\\"$pr_files\\\\\\\" | grep -E '^docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[A-Za-z][^/]*/DISPATCH-NOTE.md$' | tail -n1 || true)\\\\\\\". No assertion that exactly one match was found. If two DISPATCH-NOTEs appear (e.g., 024C and 024D in a squash-merge scenario), enforcement is applied only to 024D.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A PR containing two cohort cascade slices could have one slice's cascade-status unchecked. The invocation-log audit trail invariant (invariant 2) would be unenforceable for the silently-skipped slice. Practical risk is low (cascade slices are single-PR by protocol) but the contract is unguarded.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"After the grep, assert the count is exactly 1; if count > 1 emit an error and set enforce=false with a diagnostic, or fail the step. Example: count=$(printf '%s\\\\\\\\n' \\\\\\\"$pr_files\\\\\\\" | grep -Ec '…'); if [ \\\\\\\"$count\\\\\\\" -gt 1 ]; then echo 'Multiple DISPATCH-NOTEs found; ambiguous enforcement' >&2; exit 1; fi\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-024B-EXTRAS-004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_restore_source_json schema validator does not include required_signatures in its required_types type-check pass. A malformed pre-state JSON with required_signatures as a non-dict (e.g. the string 'true') passes schema validation but causes an AttributeError in the downstream (data.get('required_signatures') or {}).get('enabled', False) snippet at line 617.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/enable-required-check.sh lines 345-357: required_types dict validates required_status_checks, enforce_admins, required_pull_request_reviews, restrictions — but NOT required_signatures. Lines 612-621: separate Python snippet assumes required_signatures is a dict or absent. If required_signatures is a truthy non-dict (e.g. a string), the or {} branch is bypassed and .get() raises AttributeError.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Fail-safe (script exits non-zero via set -e on the Python exception), but the error message is a Python traceback rather than a human-readable schema validation error. Operator sees confusing output when the real fix is the pre-state JSON file.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add required_signatures: (dict, type(None)) to required_types in validate_restore_source_json, or explicitly validate its type in the separate Python snippet before the .get() call.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CORR-024B-EXTRAS-005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"D-047 row describes the CI workflow trigger as using 'paths-ignore: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**' but does not acknowledge the co-spec paths: filter that was added, which is the root of finding CORR-024B-EXTRAS-001. The decision record is incomplete as a description of the workflow trigger shape.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"docs/DECISIONS.md line 93: D-047 rationale text says 'wires the CLI gate into PR checks with paths-ignore: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**'. The implemented workflow (.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 17-22) also adds a paths: filter with three entries. D-047 does not mention the paths: filter.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Future operators reading D-047 would not understand the intended trigger shape and could not reconstruct why both filters were present. Increases confusion when diagnosing CORR-024B-EXTRAS-001.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"After fixing CORR-024B-EXTRAS-001, update D-047 rationale to describe the final trigger shape (paths-only or paths-ignore-only) accurately.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"6e843d6d-330e-44fc-a0e7-32d2be710f4d\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.2235101,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":14,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":124216,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":837367,\"output_tokens\":33639,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":124216,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":12235,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":126108,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":5489,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":5489},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1753,\"outputTokens\":22,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001863,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":14,\"outputTokens\":33639,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":837367,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":124216,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.2216471,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"ffa2c255-532b-4c47-acc6-f393b7d230d5\"}\n\n---\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":805079,\"duration_api_ms\":805524,\"num_turns\":32,\"result\":\"I now have a comprehensive view of the implementation after all fix rounds. Let me produce the findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCK\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024 024B-extras\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"All CRIT and MAJ findings from prior rounds (R1–R4) are verified closed. SAFE-R3-001 (OPERATOR unbound before GATE2_CAUTION_LINE) is fixed: OPERATOR is now set at line 806 before assembly at line 808. SAFE-R3-002 (committed-deletion bypass) is fixed: git log -p now covers history and the enable-committed-history-prior-restore test verifies it. SAFE-R3-003 (GITHUB_ACTIONS not unset in run_restore_case) is fixed. SAFE-R3-004 (required_status_checks null rejection) is fixed with (dict, type(None)) typing and null transform. SAFE-R3-007 (Gate 3 command fragment) is fixed: ADOPT-001 §12.8 now shows full invocations for both Gate 3 paths. Two MAJ findings block merge: SAFE-R3-005 (carry-forward) — the --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification happy-path remains untested; the escape hatch code path has no regression guard after fix-round fixes. SAFE-NEW-001 — the CI workflow uses tail -n1 to select a single DISPATCH-NOTE from all matching paths in the PR diff; a PR with both a genuine cascade DISPATCH-NOTE and an alphabetically-later decoy causes enforcement to run against the decoy, bypassing the onboarded-operator-bump TF-row requirement. Two MIN findings remain: SAFE-R3-006 (carry-forward, per_page=100 pagination blocks Gate 3 re-enable for estates with >100 tags) and SAFE-NEW-002 (restrictions passthrough in validate_restore_source_json). No descoping recommended.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-R3-005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification happy-path (prior restore evidence present + no --expected-wrapper-sha → exit 0 + CAUTION log block) has no test. The only test using this flag (line 2617) hits the mutex guard before check_forward_mode_safety runs. GATE2_CAUTION_LINE emission in the audit log block is not regression-guarded.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh:2617 — sole usage exercises --expected-wrapper-sha + --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification mutex (exit 2). enable-required-check.sh:487-504 — when prior_restore_evidence_present=true + EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA=empty + ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1, the function returns 0 silently. GATE2_CAUTION_LINE assembly at line 808 and log-block inclusion at line 874 are never exercised by any test.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Any regression in the escape hatch path (CAUTION silently dropped, unexpected exit 2) is invisible to CI. During a production outage when Gate 3 with --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification is the only viable path, the operator discovers a broken escape hatch mid-incident — violating the <30-min break-glass SLO and leaving no audit trail.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add run_enable_case: prior restore evidence committed + tags.json with v1.2.3 + no --expected-wrapper-sha + --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification → exit 0, 'CAUTION' or 'Gate 2 verification bypassed' in stdout, GATE2_CAUTION_LINE in log block output. Also test the variant where prior restore evidence is absent + flag is passed → function still exits 0 (flag is no-op without prior evidence, CAUTION correctly fires anyway per line 515).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-NEW-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The CI workflow resolves the active cascade DISPATCH-NOTE via tail -n1 across all matching paths in the PR diff. A PR containing both a genuine cascade DISPATCH-NOTE (e.g. 024C/DISPATCH-NOTE.md with cascade-status: onboarded-operator-bump) and an alphabetically-later decoy (e.g. 024C-workaround/DISPATCH-NOTE.md with cascade-status: onboarded + valid log entry) causes enforcement to run against the decoy, bypassing the onboarded-operator-bump STATUS.md TF-row requirement.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.yml:46 — 'dispatch_note=$(... grep ... | tail -n1 || true)'. No uniqueness guard. For a PR with 024C/DISPATCH-NOTE.md (missing TF row) + 024C-workaround/DISPATCH-NOTE.md (cascade-status: onboarded, matching log entry), alphabetical sort places 024C-workaround last; tail -n1 selects it; CI enforces against the decoy and exits 0. tests: no test covers multiple DISPATCH-NOTE.md files in one PR.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Invariant 2 R-024-07 closure (TF-024X-renovate-<slug> STATUS.md row requirement for onboarded-operator-bump) is bypassed. A cascade slice declaring onboarded-operator-bump without the required TF row can merge through CI by including a decoy DISPATCH-NOTE. Threshold A tracking is silently corrupted for that adopter.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"After filtering for WP-SCP-024 DISPATCH-NOTE paths, count matches. If count > 1, exit non-zero with 'expected exactly one cascade DISPATCH-NOTE in PR diff, found N'. Add test: fake gh returning two DISPATCH-NOTE paths → workflow step fails. Same guard should be added to check-invocation-log-entry.sh when dispatched via --pr mode for defense-in-depth.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-R3-006\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags fetches the tag list with per_page=100 and no pagination loop. An SCP repo with >100 tags truncates the list; valid release-tag SHAs for older tags are not found, causing exit 2 with 'not found in release-tag SHA cache' and blocking Gate 3 re-enable.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh:443 — gh api 'repos/jrnb2024/standards-control-plane/git/refs/tags?per_page=100'. No Link-header pagination. GitHub REST API caps per_page at 100 items/page. Fails safe (rejects valid SHA rather than accepting invalid SHA) but blocks emergency recovery when estate accumulates tags.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Gate 3 re-enable permanently fails for operators whose valid release-tag SHA maps to a tag beyond position 100. Operator is forced to use --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification, increasing break-glass risk. Violates <30-min SLO when the escape hatch itself has the test gap from SAFE-R3-005.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a pagination loop over Link: <next> headers, or switch to GraphQL refs API with cursor pagination. Alternatively document the <100-tag assumption explicitly with a TODO comment and a test that verifies behaviour when tags.json has exactly 100 entries.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-NEW-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"validate_restore_source_json strips _links, url, checks, required_signatures from the GET-shape but passes restrictions verbatim. A crafted before-JSON with restrictions: {users: [...], teams: [], apps: []} passes schema validation and is PUT to the branch-protection API, adding push restrictions not present in the canonical SCP posture. Posture-degradation guards check only enforce_admins and required_status_checks.contexts, not restrictions.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh:327-377 — transform function: 'if key in {\\\\\\\"_links\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"url\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"checks\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"required_signatures\\\\\\\"}: continue'. restrictions is not in the exclusion set. required_types: restrictions: (dict, type(None)) — valid if dict. A before-JSON with restrictions: {users: [X], teams: [], apps: []} passes type check and passes through to PUT body. Lines 646-653 — posture-degradation checks only test enforce_admins + contexts count.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence_location\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh:362-375 (transform), 344-356 (schema check), 646-653 (posture-degradation guards)\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A forged or manually edited pre-state JSON can add branch push restrictions that lock out the operator from direct pushes. Requires local filesystem write access to the pre-state JSON; bounded to the operator's own machine in single-operator mode (D-031). Not a remote attack surface.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add restrictions to the posture-degradation check: if restrictions is non-null, require an explicit --i-understand-restore-adds-push-restrictions flag. Alternatively strip or null restrictions in the transform when the source is the SCP canonical posture (which sets restrictions: null). Add a test fixture with non-null restrictions expecting the degradation guard to fire.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-NEW-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE §scope table describes the CI workflow paths-ignore as including 'docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md' and 'STATUS.md'. The actual workflow has those files in paths: (triggers, not ignores). D-047 row text is correct (only mentions 024[AB]*/**). The DISPATCH-NOTE description creates a misleading operational picture of which files can bypass CI triggering.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE.md:38 — 'paths-ignore: [\\\\\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"STATUS.md\\\\\\\"]'. check-invocation-log-entry.yml:17-22 — paths: includes branch-protection-log.md + STATUS.md; paths-ignore: only has 024[AB]*/**. D-047 DECISIONS.md row accurately says paths-ignore covers only 024[AB]*/**.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator reading the DISPATCH-NOTE could incorrectly conclude that branch-protection-log.md changes alone cannot trigger the CI workflow, potentially missing a CI run on a PR that only modifies the log file. No actual bypass; the implementation is correct.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Correct the DISPATCH-NOTE paths-ignore description to remove branch-protection-log.md and STATUS.md. Clarify that those files are in paths: (triggers) so a PR touching only those files triggers the workflow and the job body short-circuits on tooling slices.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"SAFE-NEW-004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"When --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification is passed but no prior restore evidence exists, GATE2_CAUTION_LINE is still populated and included in the invocation log block, producing a misleading 'Gate 3 invoked with --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification — wrapper SHA NOT verified' audit entry even when no Gate 2 bypass was needed or performed.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh:807-810 — GATE2_CAUTION_LINE assembly is unconditional on ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1, no guard for prior restore evidence. check_forward_mode_safety:487-503 — the bypass only matters when prior_restore_evidence_present=true; when false, the function returns 0 normally without any SHA check. The CAUTION line fires regardless.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Audit trail contains a misleading bypass annotation when no bypass was needed. Post-incident forensic analysis of the log may incorrectly flag a routine forward-mode enable as having bypassed Gate 2 verification. Minor audit noise.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Condition GATE2_CAUTION_LINE assembly on both ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1 AND prior_restore_evidence_present returning true. Or emit a less alarming log line when the flag was provided but prior evidence was absent (e.g., 'note: --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification provided but no prior restore evidence found for this target').\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"carry_forward_verification\\\": {\\n    \\\"R8-SAFE-001\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — ADOPT-001 §12.8:1235 has WARNING callout; §12.8:1244 has CAUTION callout for --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification; enable-required-check.sh:808-810 assembles GATE2_CAUTION_LINE AFTER OPERATOR is set at line 806 (SAFE-R3-001 fix); check_forward_mode_safety:497-499 emits CAUTION via log() when EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA is provided; D-047 DECISIONS.md row acknowledges escape hatch.\\\",\\n    \\\"R8-SAFE-002\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — prior_restore_evidence_present (lines 413-437) combines committed_blob + git diff HEAD (working tree) + git log -p (committed history); awk strips +/- prefixes so deleted restore entries remain detectable in history. Test enable-committed-history-prior-restore (line 2346) commits restore evidence, then commits a deletion, and verifies exit 2 with 'prior --restore evidence detected'.\\\",\\n    \\\"R8-Bootstrap\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — check-invocation-log-entry.yml paths-ignore: docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/** covers 024A, 024B-core, 024B-extras; job body case statement exits 0 for 024A|024B-core|024B-extras|024G; defense-in-depth provided by two independent layers.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-001\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — enable-required-check.sh:806 sets OPERATOR via gh api user; GATE2_CAUTION_LINE assembled at line 808 AFTER OPERATOR is populated. No unbound variable risk under set -euo pipefail.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-002\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — git log -p in prior_restore_evidence_present covers committed history; test enable-committed-history-prior-restore verifies committed deletion does not bypass the check.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-003\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — run_restore_case (line 377) and run_enable_case (line 436) both include 'unset CI GITHUB_ACTIONS' in the subshell before invoking RESTORE_SCRIPT.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-004\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — validate_restore_source_json required_types: required_status_checks: (dict, type(None)); transform branch for null at lines 367-368 produces PUT-safe {'strict': False, 'contexts': []}; test restore-null-status-checks (line 1921) verifies correct behavior.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-005\\\": \\\"OPEN — see finding SAFE-R3-005 above.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-006\\\": \\\"OPEN — see finding SAFE-R3-006 above.\\\",\\n    \\\"SAFE-R3-007\\\": \\\"VERIFIED CLOSED — ADOPT-001 §12.8 Gate 3 (lines 1226-1231) now shows full 'scripts/enable-required-check.sh --repo ... --branch ... --expected-wrapper-sha <sha>' command; bypass variant (lines 1237-1241) shows full 'scripts/enable-required-check.sh --repo ... --branch ... --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification' command.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"97c5df2f-0511-49c1-a665-a7f26134d695\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.9474320000000005,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":25,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":148754,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":2489885,\"output_tokens\":42718,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":148754,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":8462,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":155711,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":424,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":424},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1669,\"outputTokens\":25,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001794,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":25,\"outputTokens\":42718,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":2489885,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":148754,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.9456380000000002,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"41fe0512-9d59-4f01-bc32-5a9a46747454\"}\n\n---\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":476888,\"duration_api_ms\":476107,\"num_turns\":17,\"result\":\"Now I have enough information to produce the structured findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer_id\\\": \\\"COMPLETENESS_GOVERNANCE_R1\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-extras\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"NEEDS_FIXES\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Slice 024B-extras delivers rollback + break-glass + CI integration deferred from 024B-core. The overall implementation is solid: all eight DISPATCH-NOTE ACs map to concrete artefacts; invariant-2 CI-enforced component closes via the wired workflow; invariant-7 rollback SLO is addressed pending step-7 demo; VERSIONING.md carries all four new flags; D-047 is filed; ADOPT-001 §12.8 3-gate playbook is concrete and non-advisory; carry-forward R8-CORR-001/003 confirmed closed in 024B-core, R8-CORR-002/SAFE-001/SAFE-002 addressed in this slice. Step-7 demo correctly remains unchecked at R1 with TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 filed as merge-blocking. Anti-descoping posture is clean — no silent drops. Two MAJ gaps require fixes before fixpoint: the merge-blocking placeholder evidence file listed in the DISPATCH-NOTE scope table is absent, and FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001 remains OPEN despite 024B-extras being the stated closure slice (D-044 'must' clause for --tooling-slice-id hardcoding not fulfilled; alternate mechanism used without explanation). Two MIN gaps: GitHub Actions prohibits paths+paths-ignore on the same event (dead config, security property maintained by job-body); D-047 prose does not mirror D-044 structured format.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Merge-blocking placeholder evidence file absent.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE scope table lists `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md` as a Codex deliverable for this slice. STATUS.md says 'placeholder path recorded' at that path. Glob and directory inspection show the file does not exist. Git status for the 024B-extras directory shows no placeholder file entry.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 (merge-blocking per plan-doc §6 + 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003) has no designated landing zone. The operator has no file to replace with real evidence at step 7; the AC cannot be checked off without a pre-existing placeholder artifact.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Create `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-extras/restore-roundtrip-evidence-PLACEHOLDER.md` with a skeleton: placeholder header, fixture steps, evidence fields (`pre-state captured`, `--restore invoked`, `after-state match assertion`, `byte-for-byte diff: clean`), and a merge-blocking note. Codex dispatch can create this; operator fills it at step 7.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MAJ-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001 not closed per D-044 'must' clause; --tooling-slice-id hardcoding absent.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"D-044 limitation note (DECISIONS.md final line): '024B-extras must hardcode --tooling-slice-id per workflow file ... that workflow-side hardcoding is the canonical closure path.' Actual `.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` invokes `check-invocation-log-entry.sh` without `--tooling-slice-id` or `--allow-not-applicable`. Tooling-slice bypass is handled by a job-body bash case-statement before the script call. STATUS.md still lists FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001 as OPEN without explanation of alternate approach.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"The D-044-specified defense-in-depth layer is absent. If a future tooling slice ID is not added to the job-body case statement, `cascade-status: not applicable` will fail closed without the script-level safety valve that `--tooling-slice-id` provides. Governance audit trail is inconsistent: FUP OPEN + D-044 'must' unmet + no rationale for the alternate mechanism.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Either (a) add `--tooling-slice-id` as a hardcoded flag in the workflow's script invocation step with the appropriate tooling slice enumeration (closes D-044 literally), or (b) explicitly close FUP-024B-CORE-NOT-APPLICABLE-WORKFLOW-001 in STATUS.md with a rationale explaining why the job-body short-circuit is an acceptable substitute and amend the D-044 limitation note accordingly. Option (a) is the prescribed path.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"paths + paths-ignore on the same pull_request event is unsupported per GitHub Actions documentation; paths-ignore is dead configuration.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"`.github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml` specifies both `paths:` and `paths-ignore:` under the same `pull_request:` trigger. GitHub Actions documentation: 'You cannot use both on.<pull_request>.paths and on.<pull_request>.paths-ignore for the same event.' paths-ignore is silently ignored. Additionally, the DISPATCH-NOTE spec listed `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md` and `STATUS.md` as paths-ignore entries; these are absent from the actual workflow.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Security property is maintained by the job-body bash short-circuit (which correctly exits 0 for tooling slices). But STATUS.md-only PRs trigger an unnecessary CI run (job short-circuits immediately on no cascade DISPATCH-NOTE). Documentation is misleading: spec says paths-ignore provides bootstrap protection, but it has no effect. Future contributors may rely on paths-ignore and miss that the job-body is the actual guard.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Remove the `paths-ignore:` block (it has no effect alongside `paths:`). The job-body short-circuit is the actual protection. Add a comment in the workflow explaining that tooling-slice detection is done in the Resolve step. Optionally, use `!` prefix exclusion patterns inside `paths:` if GitHub supports this pattern for PR events.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"D-047 does not mirror D-044's structured multi-component format as specified by DISPATCH-NOTE.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE review checklist item: 'D-047 row. Format mirrors D-044.' D-044 entry uses numbered component structure (1), (2), (3) with each deliverable enumerated. D-047 is a single dense prose paragraph without component enumeration. Both cover multiple deliverables (restore mode, break-glass, workflow wiring) but D-047's structure makes the scope boundary between components harder to audit.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Minor governance-doc inconsistency. No functional impact; D-047 content is substantively complete. Harder for future reviewers to verify which D-047 clause covers which deliverable when auditing the decision log.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Restructure D-047 rationale to enumerate the three operational contracts as numbered components matching D-044's pattern: (1) --restore mode contract, (2) ADOPT-001 §12.8 break-glass gates, (3) CI workflow wiring + bootstrap guard. One sentence per component is sufficient.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"291451f4-3266-4b1f-ab5b-78c1404eb734\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.03539955,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1072,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":134347,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":584481,\"output_tokens\":23407,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":134347,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":9450,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":133316,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":8412,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":8412},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1823,\"outputTokens\":22,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001933,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":1072,\"outputTokens\":23407,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":584481,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":134347,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.03346655,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"9b30b872-d6bb-4f1c-86d0-d0ce27c58c05\"}\n\n---\n# --restore real-repo round-trip evidence (PLACEHOLDER)\n\n**Status:** PLACEHOLDER - operator-interactive demo at dispatch-plan step 7 has NOT yet run. Merge-blocking AC per WP-SCP-024 plan-doc §6 + 024A R1 MAJ-SAFE-003.\n\n**To close this placeholder:**\n1. Operator creates a throw-away test repo (e.g. jrnb2024/scp-024b-extras-restore-test).\n2. Configure adopter wrapper + run forward-mode enable-required-check.sh.\n3. Capture pre-state JSON via the prior invocation log entry.\n4. Mutate branch protection (e.g. via gh CLI) to known different state.\n5. Run `--restore <pre-state.json>` mode.\n6. Verify branch-protection API GET response matches captured before-state byte-for-byte (jq diff).\n7. Replace this file's content with the demo log + diff output + operator sign-off + timestamp.\n\nTracked at TF-024B-STEP7-DEMO-001 (STATUS.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
